<Context title="Two Greek texts behind our Bibles" label="Guide" lastChecked="2026-11-20" sources={sources}
  description="Why the Bible your church already reads often differs from the UBS Greek New Testament, and which Greek text each Indian-language Bible follows.">
  <p class="lead">A pastor opens his Bible at Acts 8:37 and reads the verse. Your new draft, made from the UBS Greek New Testament, has no verse 37. Neither side has made a mistake. The two Bibles were translated from two different Greek texts.</p>

  <h2>Two printed Greek texts</h2>
  <p>A <em>base text</em> is the Greek text a translation is made from. Most Bibles in use today come from one of two kinds of base text.</p>
  <p>The first is the <em>Textus Receptus</em> (Latin for "received text", often shortened to TR). In 1516, in Basel, Erasmus printed the first published Greek New Testament. He used a small number of late Greek manuscripts, from about the 10th to the 15th century. Later printers corrected and reprinted his text. This line of printed editions is the TR. So there is not one single TR, but a family of close editions. The TR is near to the <a href={url('w/byz/')}>Byzantine text</a>, the form of the text found in most late manuscripts, but it is not exactly the same. The King James Version, Luther's German Bible and the Russian Synodal Bible were translated from it.</p>
  <p>The second is the <em>critical text</em>: an edition made by comparing many manuscripts, including the oldest ones found since the 19th century. The UBS Greek New Testament and the Nestle–Aland edition print the same critical text. The <a href={url('background/nt-text/')}>history of the New Testament text</a> tells this story in more detail.</p>

  <h2>Where they differ</h2>
  <p>In most verses the two texts say the same thing. But in a known set of places the TR has words, or whole verses, that the critical text leaves out of the main text. The UBS editors judged that those words were added by copyists after the first centuries. In a UBS Greek New Testament these verses are missing from the text and are given in the apparatus (the notes at the foot of the page). That is why some verse numbers seem to "jump" in a modern translation: Matthew 18:10 is followed by 18:12.</p>

  <h2>Which text each Indian-language Bible follows</h2>
  <p>To find out, we checked fifteen test verses in each version. Each of these verses is printed in the TR but left out of the critical text. A version that has the verse follows the TR at that point. The check looks only at whether the verse is there, and whether the version itself puts it in brackets or a footnote. It does not judge wording, so it works the same way in any script.</p>
  <p class="small">The fifteen test verses: {tests.join(' · ')}.</p>
  <p>To test the method, it was first run on two English Bibles whose base text is known. The King James Version had all 15 verses. The Berean Study Bible, made from the critical text, had none.</p>

