  <p class="small muted">"Follows" is inferred from these 15 verses only. It is a strong sign, not a full study of each version. Telugu, Odia and other languages have not been checked yet.</p>

  <h2>What the table shows</h2>
  <p>Every Indian-language version checked so far stands mainly on the TR side. Kannada 1951 is the exception: five test verses are missing, so it cannot be given one label. If your project works beside it, check the verse in front of you.</p>
  <p>"Follows the TR" does not mean every version is the same. 1 John 5:7–8 has a longer form in the TR, which speaks of "the Father, the Word, and the Holy Spirit" in heaven. Almost no Greek manuscript has it. The Tamil Old Version prints it; the Malayalam 1910 version does not. Within Malayalam, Bailey's New Testament of 1829, the first in the language, has it, and the 1910 revision dropped it. A base text can change between one edition and the next.</p>
  <p>Several versions already mark the doubtful verses themselves. The Hindi Old Version puts twelve in brackets with a footnote. When you discuss one of these verses with a review committee, you can begin with the note their own Bible already prints.</p>

  <h2>Four passages the table does not score</h2>
  <p>Mark 16:9–20, Luke 22:43–44, John 7:53–8:11 and 1 John 5:7–8 were noted but not counted. The UBS text prints the first three in double brackets, and versions on both sides may print or footnote them, so finding them in a Bible does not show which Greek text it follows.</p>

  <h2>The case for the TR and the Byzantine text</h2>
  <p>Most textual critics today hold that the oldest manuscripts usually keep the earliest wording, and the UBS committee works this way. A minority of scholars disagree. Some hold that the text found in the great majority of manuscripts, the Byzantine or "Majority" text, is closest to the original, because God preserved it in the copies the church used for centuries. Others defend the TR itself, often for the same reason. People in your churches who hold these views are not ignorant. They are taking one side of a real debate, and they deserve a careful answer, not a dismissal.</p>
  <p>Note also that the TR and the Majority text are not the same. In some places, 1 John 5:7–8 among them, the TR has a reading that most Byzantine manuscripts do not have.</p>

  <h2>What this means for your work</h2>
  <p>If you translate from the UBS text in a language whose older Bible follows the TR, your draft and your readers' Bible will disagree at these verses. This will happen many times, not once. It helps to agree with the project's sponsors before drafting how you will handle such verses: leave them out, put them in brackets, or keep them with a footnote. Then the decision is not made again, under pressure, verse by verse.</p>

